  "title": "Introducing useState",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Build your First Web App with React"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Introducing useState"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "React gives you a hook called useState to add state to a component."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You call it with the initial value of the state. It returns two things: the current value, and a function to update it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "An example is given below:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "const [entries, setEntries] = useState([]);"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "entries is the current value (an array). We start with an empty array [] as the initial value, which is returned to entries.",
        "setEntries is the function you will call when you want to change the array (for example when adding an entry)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Important Note:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You must call useState at the top level of your component, not inside a loop or an if statement."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "That's because React uses the order of hooks to keep the state correct. You'll learn more about this in a future course."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
